summary: MLS Policy Investigation Plan
description: |
    The initial problem was thought to be an infrastructure limitation
    preventing MLS policy testing. However, experimentation has shown that
    the issue lies within the selinux-policy itself, specifically with
    missing rules for cloud-init in the MLS policy.

    A candidate fix lives on JJanasek/selinux-policy@mls-enable-cloud-init
    (https://github.com/JJanasek/selinux-policy/pull/new/mls-enable-cloud-init):
    it enables the cloudform module for the mls policy store. Since
    cloudform doesn't exist in the stock mls store at all yet, this plan
    builds it as a standalone third-party module with the standard
    `/usr/share/selinux/devel/Makefile` workflow (the same one
    `policycoreutils-devel` documents for building a single module against
    whatever policy is actually installed) and adds it to the guest's live
    mls store with `semodule -i`, before relabeling/rebooting, so we can
    confirm the fix clears the cloud-init AVC denials. The module's source
    files (cloudform.te/if/fc plus the new NetworkManager and ssh
    interfaces) are checked into this plan under mls-cloudinit-fix/,
    mirroring the fork commits, so the build has no runtime dependency on
    GitHub.

    The first pass of this fix only added NetworkManager/net_conf rules
    and got cloud-init transitioning into cloud_init_t for the first time
    (previously it silently ran unconfined as init_t, because cloudform.te
    relies on `unconfined_domain(cloud_init_t)` for targeted/mcs, which
    isn't enabled under mls). That surfaced a much larger set of small,
    previously-invisible gaps in cloudform.te's own ruleset -- dhcpcd pid/
    signal handling, systemd unit-file status queries, udev trigger/settle,
    ssh host key reads and ~/.ssh management, shadow reads for the
    set-passwords module, and locale/timezone file management -- which
    the second pass adds. A third pass, after re-running with only the
    prior two passes' rules and diffing the fresh denial set, closes three
    more boot-to-boot-variable gaps that hadn't shown up in every run:
    cloud-init's own stage-synchronization sockets under
    /run/cloud-init/share/*.sock are a distinct sock_file object class
    from the plain files sysnet_manage_config() already covers (needs its
    own sysnet_manage_config_sockets() interface), `setgid` (dropping to
    the `adm` group before writing command-output logs) is a distinct
    capability from the ones already granted, and `systemd-detect-virt`'s
    container check needs nsfs file getattr.

    `storage_raw_read_fixed_disk()` hitting a raw block device labeled
    s15 while cloud_init_t runs at s0 turned out to be a genuine MLS BLP
    "no read up" constraint (`mlsconstrain`), not a missing TE rule --
    `audit2allow`'s suggested `allow` for it is a no-op, since the
    constraint check is independent of and in addition to normal TE
    rules. The fourth pass makes the deliberate call to fix this: growpart
    (cc_growpart.py, run in cloud_init_t itself since it has no dedicated
    domain) legitimately needs to read the raw disk to grow the root
    filesystem regardless of its labeled sensitivity, so cloud_init_t is
    declared MLS-trusted for file reads at all levels via
    `mls_file_read_all_levels()` -- the first domain in the tree to use
    that attribute.

    Tried switching to real enforcing mode to test acceptance criterion
    1 directly, expecting the earlier unlabeled_t denials to be a
    transient relabel-vs-early-boot race that a *second* reboot (without
    touching /.autorelabel again, so no relabel runs the second time)
    would dodge. It didn't: the second, already-fully-relabeled boot hit
    the exact same /etc/ld.so.cache denial, now enforced for real, which
    cascades into udev helpers failing to exec (can't map their own
    ld.so.cache) -> disk-by-uuid symlinks never appearing -> local-fs
    mount timeouts -> emergency mode -- and emergency mode is a dead end
    too, since root is locked, so the guest hangs forever and tmt's
    reconnect times out. So this was never a race; the file's on-disk
    context is durably wrong (`unconfined_u:object_r:ld_so_cache_t:s0`
    -- a leftover *targeted*-policy context; unconfined_u isn't even a
    valid mls user).

    Root-caused it: plain restorecon/setfiles (as run by the standard
    boot-time `/.autorelabel` relabel) only fix a file's *type*, never
    its user/role/range, unless given `-F`. Since `ld_so_cache_t` is the
    same type under both targeted and mls, the relabel sees "type
    already correct" and leaves the stale targeted-policy user in
    place forever -- confirmed directly: plain `restorecon` reports no
    change, `restorecon -F` fixes it (`unconfined_u` -> `system_u`).
    `prepare_for_mls.sh` now does a forced, full-context
    `restorecon -RF /` after the reboot (`prepare_for_mls_force_relabel`)
    to fix this for every file, not just ld.so.cache -- necessary since
    installing/configuring anything before the reboot (which is
    unavoidable) leaves files with the old policy's user on disk.

    With ld.so.cache fixed, the next enforcing boot got further but hit
    a different hang: local-fs.target failed waiting on /dev/disk/by-uuid
    symlinks that udev never produced, landing in emergency mode (a dead
    end -- no sshd, root locked). Marking every systemd `*_generator_t`
    domain permissive (in case the generator-stage kmsg/bash denials were
    the cause) didn't change this outcome, so those were a red herring.
    Static review of udev_t/fsadm_t/lvm_t shows they're already heavily
    MLS-trusted (`mls_file_read_all_levels` and friends) in the stock
    policy, so the cause isn't obvious from source alone, and the
    console log can't show it either (`kauditd_printk_skb: N callbacks
    suppressed` -- the real denial(s) never made it to the console
    before auditd was up to drain them). To get real evidence instead of
    guessing further, the plan now makes the enforcing-mode local-fs
    failure survivable (temporary `nofail` on non-root fstab entries) and
    makes sure the underlying AVCs actually get captured
    (`audit_backlog_limit=8192`, up from the kernel default of 64), then
    inspects the loaded policy and captured denials directly.

    `nofail` alone got further (local-fs.target/sysinit.target were
    actually reached this time) but still wasn't enough: other services
    (systemd-logind, chronyd, ...) directly require var.mount/home.mount
    regardless of local-fs.target, and NetworkManager.service separately
    hung for its own ~11-minute internal timeout -- plausibly because it
    discovers interfaces via a udev monitor and never got told the NIC
    exists, same as the disk symlinks. There's also no interactive
    console available to log in locally once the guest lands in
    emergency mode, only a passive console-log tail. So this plan now
    bypasses udev and NetworkManager for networking entirely (using only
    /sys/class/net, populated directly by the kernel driver, plus a raw
    dhclient) and gives sshd its own dependency-free path to start, purely
    to reach a live shell and finally capture the real blocking AVC(s)
    with ausearch instead of guessing from console output.

    That escape hatch reached sshd.service ("Started sshd.service"), but
    the raw dhclient bring-up it depends on failed too -- its own
    device-probe loop over /sys/class/net never found a non-loopback
    interface within 30s, so sshd had no route and stayed unreachable.
    The guest then just sat in this broken state until Testing Farm's own
    infrastructure-level watchdog force-rebooted it (repeating in a clean
    ~5.5-minute cycle, confirmed by diffing four consecutive console-log
    boot segments with byte-identical failure signatures), until tmt's
    own `tmt-reboot -t 1200` budget ran out and the plan errored with
    `RebootTimeoutError`.

    Before escalating further on the enforcing-mode side (console-only
    visibility, no live shell, 5-6 minutes per iteration), a cheaper
    experiment first: redo the *permissive*-mode boot (SSH stays up, so
    `ausearch` is available directly, no rate-limited-console guessing)
    but this time with `semanage dontaudit off` and the higher
    `audit_backlog_limit` active from before boot, plus debug-level
    logging enabled for systemd-udevd specifically
    (`SYSTEMD_LOG_LEVEL=debug` via a service drop-in). If the enforcing
    hang were caused by a normal TE gap, permissive mode would already
    have logged it (just not blocked it) in every prior successful
    permissive run -- it never did. Two explanations survive that: either
    the missing denial is hidden by a `dontaudit` rule (invisible in
    *both* modes, hence `dontaudit off` here), or this isn't an
    audit-visible permission check at all but a genuine non-SELinux
    systemd/udev-vs-MLS-range interaction (hence the debug logging, which
    doesn't depend on AVCs existing).     A `journald` forward-to-console
    drop-in is also staged now (harmless under permissive, since we have
    SSH there anyway) so that if this probe comes back empty, the very
    next enforcing attempt automatically gets the same visibility on the
    console without needing another round-trip to add it.

    The probe came back empty: `sesearch --dontaudit` confirmed the
    rebuild actually dropped every dontaudit rule (0, down from several
    thousand), and the only udev_t/kernel_t hits in the full,
    dontaudit-off `ausearch` dump are ordinary process-transition noise
    (siginh/rlimitinh/noatsecure) from `systemctl restart
    systemd-udevd` -- nothing resembling a blocked disk/NIC access. The
    debug-level systemd-udevd journal confirms why: it loads the SELinux
    database, reads every rule file, and creates the
    `/dev/disk/by-uuid/...` symlink for the root filesystem in ~11s,
    with no errors. So this was never a normal (or dontaudit-hidden)
    missing-rule/mlsconstrain denial.

    The very next enforcing attempt then showed the same thing for the
    first time directly: it reached multi-user.target, cloud-init.target,
    and a real `login:` prompt with ens5 configured over plain DHCP --
    all *before* hitting any escape-hatch machinery at all. It only broke
    later, when cloud-init's `ssh` config module (predictably) issued its
    own `systemctl restart sshd.service`; because the earlier escape-hatch
    override made sshd.service's dependencies point at
    diagnostic-network.service, that restart pulled that unit in for the
    first time all boot, and it failed outright: its dhclient invocation
    passed `-timeout 30`, but this dhclient build only recognizes
    `--timeout` (single-dash `-timeout` is rejected as "Unknown
    command"). That cascaded into a multi-user.target dependency failure
    right as cloud-init was trying to finish, which is what actually kept
    tmt from ever getting a stable reconnect.     Fixed the flag and reran: the escape hatch failed a *second*,
    different way (`ip link set "" up`: its own /sys/class/net
    device-probe loop came up empty even though ens5 had already been
    up and DHCP-configured normally for 25+ seconds by then), still
    with the same underlying pattern -- multi-user.target and a real
    login prompt reached first, broken only once cloud-init's `ssh`
    module restarted sshd and pulled the escape hatch in. Two
    independent enforcing-mode boots now agree that disk/NIC/udev work
    correctly on their own, and that the escape hatch is the only thing
    still breaking the boot, so it's removed for good: the real fix
    for the original hang was already the ld.so.cache/generator/nofail
    work above, and this diagnostic-only workaround outlived its
    purpose once that landed.

    That "removed for good" turned out to be premature: the very next
    run after that hit the disk-by-uuid hang again, now sharpened to a
    single device (dev-nvme0n1p3.device, the root EBS partition -- home/
    var/boot are plain fstab entries on that same device, not separate
    partitions) that never becomes "ready" a second time in the main
    OS's freshly-started udevd, even though it's already mounted as
    root via the initrd's udevd. Neither by-uuid-vs-raw-device-path nor
    a wider (60s) device-timeout changed anything, and static review
    showed udev_t is already about as MLS-trusted as a domain can be
    for this. Two live diagnostics (mls-udev-diag.service,
    mls-udev-monitor.service) were added to get real evidence without
    needing SSH; the monitor initially shipped with a systemd unit-file
    bug (unescaped `%s`/`%3N` in its ExecStart) that was fixed and
    verified locally but never actually run on Testing Farm.

    Running it did give the answer, just not from the monitor itself:
    the same boot's console showed selinux-autorelabel.service deciding
    *another* full relabel was needed and starting `fixfiles -T 0
    restore` at ~t=6.5s, on every single power-cycled boot, despite
    /.autorelabel being confirmed absent right before each one --
    competing for I/O on the very same nvme0n1p3 device everything else
    was timing out on, and re-mangling files' SELinux user back to
    unconfined_u mid-boot along the way (the same class of bug as the
    ld.so.cache fix, just recurring). Masking selinux-autorelabel.service
    outright (prepare_for_mls_force_relabel() already did the equivalent
    full relabel once, so a second one is pure redundant I/O) was the
    actual fix: the next run reached multi-user.target, started sshd,
    and reached network-online.target in well under two minutes, with
    dev-nvme0n1p3.device no longer timing out at all. The original
    boot-hang bug this whole investigation was chasing is fixed.

    That surfaced the next layer instead: tmt's own SSH reconnect still
    never succeeded, not because the guest didn't come up (it verifiably
    did, repeatedly, well within each power-cycle window) but because
    every single attempt failed at the bare TCP level --
    `ssh: connect to host <ip> port 22: Connection timed out` -- before
    any SSH protocol exchange even starts. The same fixed IP accepted
    SSH just fine for every earlier *permissive* reboot in the same run,
    so this is specific to the enforcing boot, not a general Testing
    Farm networking problem, and dontaudit-off ausearch shows nothing
    network-related, so it doesn't look like an SELinux denial either.
    Leading theories: a missing/late default route (would let inbound
    SYNs arrive and get SYN-ACKed but strand the reply, looking exactly
    like a client-side connect timeout), a firewall ruleset that's
    default-deny for a window or permanently on this boot, or sshd not
    actually bound where it claims. Added mls-net-diag.service (route/
    address/firewall state plus a loopback self-connect to sshd, polled
    every 15s for 5 minutes, forced to the console) to tell these apart
    without needing the very SSH access that's missing; the now-answered
    udev diagnostics are retired (kept in mls-cloudinit-fix/ for
    reference, no longer enabled) to free up console bandwidth for it.

    Ran it (Testing Farm eb2f649e): the loopback self-connect to sshd
    succeeded on every one of 20+ iterations spanning the full 5-minute
    window, so sshd itself accepting connections was never in question.
    But the same diagnostic's `ip route show`/`ip -4 addr show` came back
    essentially empty, and `ss`/`systemctl` calls in the very same run
    failed outright ("Cannot open netlink socket: Permission denied",
    "Failed to retrieve unit state: Access denied" with an explicit
    `scon=initrc_t tcon=init_t ... perm=status ... Permission denied").
    A plain ad hoc systemd service with no configured label lands in
    initrc_t under mls, which the stock policy doesn't grant
    NETLINK_ROUTE-socket or systemd-status-query access to -- so the
    "empty" routing table this round is most likely the diagnostic being
    blind under MLS, not proof the route table is actually empty. Marked
    initrc_t permissive (same pattern as the generator-domain probe
    earlier) so the next run's numbers can actually be trusted.

    Every run since then died a different way: right after the "Switch to
    Enforcing" step's own reboot (the first boot where SELinux actually
    enforces mls for real), tmt's very next reconnect-and-push cycle failed
    outright with "bash: /root/.bashrc: Permission denied" and an rsync
    mkstemp EACCES under the tmt workdir tree -- initially misread as a
    Testing Farm rsync/infra flake (it recurred identically across five
    separate runs: 2fc81438, 517da82d, 8886d239, 6148f7ed, 0a61c365, and
    again as 97baa9ee even after ruling out live-policy-reload disruption
    as the cause). The real explanation was already sitting in the
    permissive probe's own dontaudit-off AVC dump, just never scrutinized
    closely enough: `root:staff_r:staff_t` (not the intended sysadm_u/
    sysadm_r/sysadm_t) denied `read`/`open` on /root/.bashrc
    (admin_home_t) and denied `write`/`create`/`rename` on the workdir
    tree (var_t) -- logged-but-allowed under permissive (which is exactly
    why every earlier permissive boot in this whole investigation looked
    completely healthy), and a byte-for-byte match for what breaks for
    real the instant enforcing is live. Root cause: prepare_for_mls.sh's
    prepare_for_mls_configure() ran `semanage login -m -s sysadm_u root`
    *before* switching SELINUXTYPE to mls, so the mapping silently landed
    in the still-active targeted store instead of mls's own; mls's stock
    default then took over instead, landing root in staff_r/staff_t (a
    deliberately more confined role that was never going to have blanket
    admin_home_t/var_t access) instead of the intended sysadm_r/sysadm_t.
    Fixed by reordering those two lines (see prepare_for_mls.sh) so the
    login mapping lands in the correct (mls) store.

    Confirmed fixed by three separate Testing Farm runs after a5c7dfde
    (7055660d, a1724108, 34499498, 68ed863b, 75a93cfe): the "bash:
    /root/.bashrc: Permission denied" / rsync mkstemp EACCES failure that
    blocked every single run for the previous week is completely gone --
    every step through "Probe for udev/NIC AVCs" now passes reliably, and
    the very first real-enforcing-mode SSH session (post the "Switch to
    Enforcing" step's own reboot) works correctly. That confirms the
    original hypothesis in full: this whole class of failure really was a
    real, enforced AVC (root landing in the wrong, more confined SELinux
    role), invisible under permissive, blocking for real the instant
    enforcing went live -- not a coincidental non-SELinux bug.

    A new, *different* blocker now appears at exactly the same spot, three
    times in a row (34499498, 68ed863b, 75a93cfe): tmt's own reboot-polling
    logic crashes ~30-32s after the "Switch to Enforcing" step's tmt-reboot
    is issued, with "Plugin raised an unhandled exception" and a traceback
    bottoming out in tmt/guest/__init__.py's BootMarkBootTime.fetch():
    `assert stdout` failing because stdout is the empty string. This is a
    tmt/OpenSSH ControlMaster reliability gap, not an SELinux or mls.fmf
    problem: tmt's reboot-detection poll (`cat /proc/stat`, checked every
    30s) apparently can, at least right after issuing a real `reboot`,
    successfully open a channel on the still-cached multiplexed SSH
    connection and get back a clean exit 0 with *no output at all* instead
    of either real content or a clear connection failure -- and tmt's own
    boot-mark code has no defensive handling for that transient case, so
    it crashes the whole plan instead of just treating it as "reboot not
    detected yet, keep waiting" (which is exactly what happens 30s earlier
    and, presumably, would happen 30s later if given the chance). Notably
    this exact `tmt-reboot` mechanism has worked flawlessly, every single
    time, for the *other* reboots in this same plan (Relabel step, Probe
    step) across dozens of runs -- so whatever's different about this one
    specific reboot (the first one that actually flips SELINUX=enforcing,
    though the *shutdown* half still runs under the still-live, still-
    permissive old session) needs more evidence before concluding it's
    mls-specific rather than environmental. Next step: retry enough times
    to tell whether this is genuinely deterministic here or just an
    unlucky short streak, and if deterministic, consider a workaround at
    the tmt-reboot call site (e.g. a custom reboot command that guarantees
    the SSH channel itself closes before the actual reboot(8) syscall
    fires, so a stale multiplexed channel can't produce a false "success")
    rather than a tmt upstream patch, since this whole investigation
    treats a5c7dfde's login mapping bug as the actual root cause this plan
    was chasing.

prepare:
  - name: Install and configure MLS policy in Permissive mode
    how: shell
    script: |
      dnf install -y selinux-policy-mls policycoreutils-python-utils audit setools-console

      # prepare_for_mls_configure() (tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh) does the
      # correct, minimal, documented setup for switching to mls: mapping
      # root to sysadm_u, pointing SELINUXTYPE at the mls store, and
      # scheduling a full relabel. It's split from prepare_for_mls_reboot()
      # here because the next prepare step below needs to stage the
      # cloudform module fix into the (still inactive) mls store before we
      # actually reboot into it.
      source $TMT_TREE/tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh

      # New (a5c7dfde follow-up): reordering prepare_for_mls_configure() so
      # SELINUXTYPE flips to mls *before* the semanage login mapping (the
      # actual fix for the staff_t-vs-sysadm_t bug documented above) made
      # this step start failing outright, deterministically, in ~8s -- no
      # stderr captured at all under the step's normal `set -eo pipefail`
      # wrapper, just a bare nonzero exit once the function returns. Run it
      # with `set +e` here instead, just for this one call, so a failure
      # doesn't immediately abort the whole script before dumping the same
      # kind of state a live shell would let us inspect directly (store
      # existence/contents, current mode, semanage's own view of the login
      # database) -- our own equivalent of the "capture real evidence
      # instead of guessing further" approach used throughout this plan.
      set +e
      prepare_for_mls_configure
      configure_rc=$?
      set -e
      if [ "$configure_rc" -ne 0 ]; then
          echo "=== prepare_for_mls_configure failed with exit $configure_rc -- diagnostics ==="
          echo "--- /etc/selinux/config ---"
          cat /etc/selinux/config
          echo "--- /etc/selinux/mls/ (does the store exist / is it populated?) ---"
          ls -la /etc/selinux/mls/ 2>&1 || true
          ls -la /etc/selinux/mls/policy/ 2>&1 || true
          echo "--- current SELinux runtime status ---"
          getenforce 2>&1 || true
          sestatus 2>&1 || true
          echo "--- semanage login -l against the (now-selected) mls store ---"
          semanage login -l 2>&1 || true
          echo "--- semanage login -l against mls explicitly via -S, in case config-file selection is the issue ---"
          semanage -S mls login -l 2>&1 || true
          exit "$configure_rc"
      fi

      # IMPORTANT: Switch to permissive to survive the boot relabel.
      # Permissive still logs every AVC that would have been denied, so
      # this is sufficient for capturing denials without risking another
      # boot hang from a real enforcing-mode block. This is a workaround
      # for still-open bugs (see the relabel-race denials this plan
      # captures below), not part of the general/correct MLS switch
      # procedure documented in prepare_for_mls.sh, so it stays here
      # instead of in that shared library.
      sed -i 's/^SELINUX=.*/SELINUX=permissive/' /etc/selinux/config

      # IMPORTANT: cloud-init's `ssh` module deletes and regenerates the
      # SSH host keys on what it thinks is a fresh instance boot (see
      # below). If the host key changes, tmt/Testing Farm's SSH connection
      # back to this guest can fail in a way that just looks like "still
      # waiting for the guest to come back", hanging well past the reboot
      # timeout instead of failing cleanly. Tell cloud-init to keep the
      # existing keys instead of deleting them.
      mkdir -p /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d
      echo 'ssh_deletekeys: false' > /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/99-preserve-ssh-host-keys.cfg

      # Reset cloud-init's per-instance state so that on the *next* boot
      # (triggered below) systemd re-runs the full local -> init -> config
      # -> final stage sequence for real, under the correct SELinux domain
      # transitions. Manually re-invoking `cloud-init` from a login shell
      # after boot does NOT reproduce this: it skips the config/final
      # stages (most filesystem-changing modules live there) and it runs
      # in your login domain instead of the dedicated cloud-init domain(s),
      # since the domain transition only happens when systemd starts the
      # units.
      cloud-init clean --logs

  - name: Build and load cloud-init policy fix (JJanasek/selinux-policy@mls-enable-cloud-init)
    how: shell
    script: |
      dnf install -y policycoreutils-devel

      # IMPORTANT: don't rebuild the whole mls base + module set from
      # source (tried first; see git history of this file for why that
      # path -- and hot-patching individual modules from it into the stock
      # store -- both failed). cloudform doesn't exist in the installed mls
      # store at all yet, so adding it is a pure `semodule -i` of a new
      # module, not a replacement of anything -- there's no version to
      # reconcile. The standard, supported way to build *one* module like
      # this against whatever policy is actually installed is the devel
      # Makefile shipped by policycoreutils-devel/selinux-policy-devel: it
      # builds against /usr/share/selinux/devel/include, the same
      # interface headers (corenetwork.if, sysnet.if, ...) already backing
      # the live store, so there's no cross-snapshot mismatch to hit.
      #
      # cloudform.te calls our new networkmanager_manage_rw_conf,
      # ssh_read_server_keys, and sysnet_manage_config_sockets interfaces,
      # which only exist on the mls-enable-cloud-init fork's
      # networkmanager.if/ssh.if/sysnetwork.if, not in the stock headers.
      # All three interfaces just expand into ordinary allow/manage_*_pattern
      # rules against types the already-installed networkmanager/ssh/
      # sysnetwork modules provide -- so we don't need to rebuild or
      # reinstall those modules at all. The devel Makefile picks up every
      # *.if file under each header layer directory, so dropping our new
      # interfaces in as their own files alongside the stock
      # networkmanager.if/ssh.if/sysnetwork.if is enough; nothing installed
      # needs to be touched or replaced.
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/networkmanager-manage-rw-conf.if \
          /usr/share/selinux/devel/include/contrib/
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/ssh-read-server-keys.if \
          /usr/share/selinux/devel/include/contrib/
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/sysnetwork-manage-config-sockets.if \
          /usr/share/selinux/devel/include/system/

      mkdir -p /root/build && cd /root/build
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/cloudform.te .
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/cloudform.if .
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/cloudform.fc .

      make -f /usr/share/selinux/devel/Makefile cloudform.pp

      # IMPORTANT: -n / --noreload. `semodule -i` operates on the store
      # named by /etc/selinux/config's SELINUXTYPE -- which we already set
      # to "mls" in the previous prepare step -- but by *default* it also
      # pushes that store live into the running kernel right away via
      # security_load_policy(), regardless of whether that store matches
      # the policy the kernel is actually running. We're still running the
      # guest's original (targeted) boot policy at this point (the mls
      # store won't actually become the *active* kernel policy until the
      # reboot below, via /.autorelabel), so forcing the just-built mls
      # store live now would instantly apply MLS-level checks to every
      # already-running process/file still labeled under targeted contexts
      # -- which is exactly what happened the first time this was tried
      # without -n: the guest broke immediately (`/root/.bashrc: Permission
      # denied`, tmt's own log-pulling rsync failing with EACCES, ...)
      # because the live kernel was suddenly enforcing MLS semantics no
      # running context actually satisfied. -n keeps this an entirely
      # offline edit of the (currently inactive) mls store.
      semodule -n -i cloudform.pp
      semodule -l | grep '^cloudform\b'

  - name: Reboot to apply MLS and Relabel
    how: shell
    script: |
      # prepare_for_mls_reboot() (tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh) already guards
      # against tmt re-running this step from the top on every reconnect
      # (which, unguarded, requested a fresh reboot every time and never
      # converged) via TMT_REBOOT_COUNT.
      source $TMT_TREE/tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh
      prepare_for_mls_reboot

  - name: Confirm and fix the /etc/ld.so.cache invalid post-relabel context
    how: shell
    script: |
      # A second, already-fully-relabeled reboot into enforcing still hit
      # the exact same /etc/ld.so.cache denial as the very first boot --
      # so this was never a transient relabel-vs-early-boot *race* (which
      # would have cleared by the second boot); the file's on-disk
      # context is durably wrong. Captured trawcon on the enforcing run:
      #   unconfined_u:object_r:ld_so_cache_t:s0
      # unconfined_u isn't even a valid SELinux user under mls, and s0
      # alone isn't a valid mls range for a system object -- this reads
      # exactly like a leftover *targeted*-policy context that a full
      # mls relabel should have overwritten, but didn't.
      #
      # Confirmed root cause: restorecon/setfiles only fix the *type*
      # component of a context by default; fixing the user/role/range
      # too requires -F. ld_so_cache_t is the same type name under both
      # targeted and mls, so the boot-time relabel sees "type already
      # correct" and never touches the stale unconfined_u/s0 left over
      # from before the policy switch (matchpathcon -V even reports the
      # file as "verified" for the same reason). Demonstrated below, then
      # fixed properly for real via prepare_for_mls_force_relabel()
      # (tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh), which now does a forced, full-context
      # `restorecon -RF /` after the reboot -- see its comment for why
      # this can't run before the reboot.
      echo "=== current on-disk context ==="
      ls -Z /etc/ld.so.cache
      stat /etc/ld.so.cache

      echo "=== what mls file_contexts says this path should be (full context) ==="
      matchpathcon -V /etc/ld.so.cache

      echo "=== does a normal (type-only) restorecon think there's anything to fix? ==="
      restorecon -v /etc/ld.so.cache; echo "exit=$?"
      ls -Z /etc/ld.so.cache

      echo "=== full-context relabel of the whole filesystem (the actual fix) ==="
      source $TMT_TREE/tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh
      prepare_for_mls_force_relabel

      echo "=== /etc/ld.so.cache context after the forced relabel ==="
      ls -Z /etc/ld.so.cache

  - name: Probe for udev/NIC AVCs under permissive with dontaudit off
    how: shell
    script: |
      # TMT_REBOOT_COUNT is scoped to this prepare phase alone (tmt hands
      # each phase its own counter, reset to 0 on entry, and re-runs just
      # this phase's script -- not the whole plan -- after handling the
      # requested reboot), so this is enough to request the reboot
      # exactly once and run the diagnostics below only after it, with no
      # separate marker file needed.
      if [ "${TMT_REBOOT_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
          # Every successful *permissive*-mode boot so far (including the
          # relabel boot right before this step) already exercised the
          # exact same disk mounts and NIC probing that hang under
          # enforcing, and never logged a single udev/disk/NIC-related
          # denial -- permissive mode logs every denial it *would* have
          # blocked, so a normal missing-TE-rule gap would already be
          # visible here. It never has been. `dontaudit off` rebuilds the
          # loaded policy with dontaudit rules disabled, which is the one
          # thing that stays invisible in *both* permissive and enforcing
          # mode alike -- so if there's a suppressed denial hiding behind
          # one of those, this is the only way to see it without going
          # through another 5-6-minute enforcing-mode console-only round
          # trip.
          #
          # IMPORTANT (new, matches the class of bug already root-caused
          # for `semanage permissive -a` above/below -- see the eb2ade08
          # comment near the udev_t/NetworkManager_t permissive calls):
          # `semanage dontaudit off` without `--noreload`/`-N` does an
          # immediate rebuild-and-reload of the whole mls store into the
          # live kernel, right here, followed within a handful of lines by
          # this exact step's own `tmt-reboot` -- a real, hard reboot of
          # the guest. Every single run through eb2ade08 (7 in a row:
          # 2fc81438/517da82d/8886d239/6148f7ed/0a61c365/97baa9ee/37e1e883)
          # died the same way, immediately after *this* step hands back to
          # tmt: "bash: /root/.bashrc: Permission denied" plus an rsync
          # mkstemp EACCES pushing the *next* step's script over the
          # freshly-reestablished SSH session. A live diagnostic pass
          # right after that reboot (id -Z, on-disk labels/modes for the
          # failing paths, pam_namespace/namespace.conf, the full mount
          # table) found nothing actually broken -- context was a normal
          # root:staff_r:staff_t:s0-s15:c0.c1023, /root and /root/.bashrc
          # had ordinary correct labels/modes, no polyinstantiation is
          # even configured (every namespace.conf directive is stock-
          # commented-out), and root could freely write to both /root and
          # /var/ARTIFACTS moments before the very next push failed the
          # same way. That points at a timing/ordering bug, not a
          # persistent state problem: if the reboot below fires while the
          # `dontaudit off` rebuild+reload triggered here is still
          # finishing in the background (plausible under mls's much
          # larger c0.c1023 category space than targeted/mcs), the on-disk
          # store or some semanage-managed file could be left mid-write,
          # which would only show up as a downstream oddity on the very
          # next boot -- exactly where every one of those 7 runs actually
          # failed. `-N` defers the reload to the next policy load
          # (i.e. this same imminent reboot) instead of doing it live,
          # the same fix already proven for `semanage permissive -a`
          # elsewhere in this plan, and it's free here since the reboot
          # is the very next thing this step does anyway.
          semanage dontaudit -N off

          # Same audit_backlog_limit fix as the later enforcing step,
          # just moved earlier so this probe boot benefits from it too --
          # otherwise a burst of early-boot AVCs could still get dropped
          # from the backlog before auditd drains them, independent of
          # dontaudit.
          grubby --update-kernel=ALL --args="audit_backlog_limit=8192"

          # SYSTEMD_LOG_LEVEL=debug specifically for systemd-udevd (not
          # systemd itself, to avoid drowning everything else in noise):
          # covers the case this isn't a permission check at all but a
          # udev-side bug that only manifests as MLS ranges/categories
          # (e.g. failing to compute a worker or device-node context,
          # silently retrying/hanging instead of erroring) -- which
          # `ausearch` can never show since no avc_has_perm() call would
          # even happen. journald's forward-to-console is staged here too
          # (inert under permissive, since SSH already works) so the
          # *next* enforcing attempt gets the same visibility for free if
          # this probe comes back empty.
          mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/systemd-udevd.service.d
          cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/systemd-udevd-debug.conf \
              /etc/systemd/system/systemd-udevd.service.d/99-debug.conf
          mkdir -p /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d
          cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/journald-forward-console.conf \
              /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/99-diag-console.conf
          systemctl daemon-reload

          tmt-reboot -t 300
      else
          echo "=== confirm dontaudit rules are actually gone from the loaded policy ==="
          echo "dontaudit rule count (should be ~0, not the stock policy's several thousand):"
          sesearch --dontaudit 2>&1 | wc -l || true

          echo "=== sanity: did this fresh boot reach multi-user/network fine under permissive? ==="
          systemctl is-system-running 2>&1 || true
          ip addr show 2>&1 || true
          ls -la /dev/disk/by-uuid/ 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== full AVC/USER_AVC list, this (dontaudit-off) boot ==="
          ausearch -m AVC,USER_AVC -ts boot 2>&1 || echo "no AVCs at all this boot"

          echo "=== filtered: anything touching udev/NIC/module/device machinery ==="
          ausearch -m AVC,USER_AVC -ts boot 2>&1 \
              | grep -iE 'udev|nic|netif|module|kernel_t|net_device|rawip|tun_socket|ethernet|virtio|ena_|eth0|ens[0-9]|zram|blk_file|fixed_disk|disk_device' \
              || echo "none found -- this isn't a normal (or dontaudit-suppressed) TE/mlsconstrain denial"

          echo "=== systemd-udevd debug journal for this boot (SYSTEMD_LOG_LEVEL=debug) ==="
          journalctl -b -u systemd-udevd --no-pager -o short-monotonic 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== udev trigger/settle activity this boot ==="
          journalctl -b -u 'systemd-udev*' --no-pager -o short-monotonic 2>&1 || true

          # New: every run since eb2ade08 (deferred -n permissive changes)
          # still dies immediately after *this* step hands back to tmt,
          # when tmt tries to rsync-push the next step's script over this
          # same just-established SSH session -- "bash: /root/.bashrc:
          # Permission denied" plus rsync mkstemp EACCES under
          # /var/ARTIFACTS/.../tmt/mls/tree/. That's the exact symptom this
          # file already documented once before for a *different* cause
          # (a live semodule/permissive reload disrupting a running
          # session's context mid-flight) -- but no such reload happens
          # between here and the next step, so it must be something about
          # *this* session itself: this is the first SSH login to land as
          # sysadm_u/sysadm_r/sysadm_t under the now-active MLS kernel
          # policy (the semanage login mapping was staged pre-reboot but
          # could never actually take effect until the mls policy itself
          # became the live kernel policy, which only happened starting
          # with the "Reboot to apply MLS and Relabel" step). Capture
          # everything needed to explain a *DAC* (not AVC-visible, since
          # we're still permissive) Permission denied on this exact
          # session before it gets reused for the next push: our own
          # resulting context/range, on-disk labels for the paths that
          # fail, and whether pam_namespace (stock Fedora/MLS's per-level
          # polyinstantiation of /tmp, and sometimes $HOME) is configured
          # and could be bind-mounting something unexpected over /root or
          # interfering with /var/ARTIFACTS.
          echo "=== our own resulting SELinux context/range for this SSH session ==="
          id -Z 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== /root and /root/.bashrc: DAC mode/owner + SELinux label ==="
          ls -laZd /root 2>&1 || true
          ls -laZ /root/.bashrc 2>&1 || true
          stat /root/.bashrc 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== /var/ARTIFACTS and the live work directory: DAC mode/owner + SELinux label ==="
          ls -laZd /var/ARTIFACTS 2>&1 || true
          ls -laZd /var/ARTIFACTS/work-* 2>&1 || true
          find /var/ARTIFACTS -maxdepth 3 -exec ls -ldZ {} \; 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== is pam_namespace configured (stock MLS per-level /tmp or \$HOME polyinstantiation)? ==="
          grep -H pam_namespace /etc/pam.d/* 2>&1 || echo "pam_namespace not referenced in /etc/pam.d/*"
          cat /etc/security/namespace.conf 2>&1 || true
          mount 2>&1 | grep -iE 'root|artifacts|tmp-inst|polyinstant' || echo "no polyinstantiation-looking mounts found"

          echo "=== full mount table, for anything odd (bind mounts, wrong fs, ro remounts) ==="
          mount 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== sanity: can root actually write *anywhere* right now, or is this global? ==="
          touch /root/.mls-diag-write-test 2>&1 && echo "write to /root OK" && rm -f /root/.mls-diag-write-test
          touch /var/ARTIFACTS/.mls-diag-write-test 2>&1 && echo "write to /var/ARTIFACTS OK" && rm -f /var/ARTIFACTS/.mls-diag-write-test
      fi

  - name: Switch to Enforcing and reboot again (tests acceptance criterion 1 for real)
    how: shell
    script: |
      # Gated behind TEST_ENFORCING (default on now that the previous
      # step's forced relabel should have fixed the root cause of the
      # hang this step used to reproduce -- flip it off again if it
      # regresses without wanting to burn 20 minutes on every run).
      # TMT_REBOOT_COUNT is its own fresh, phase-scoped counter (see the
      # note in the probe step above), so -eq 0 here only guards *this*
      # phase's own reboot, independent of the reboot the probe step
      # above already took.
      if [ "${TEST_ENFORCING:-1}" = "1" ] && [ "${TMT_REBOOT_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
          # The ld.so.cache fix above cleared that denial, but the very
          # next enforcing boot then hung on a *different*, pre-existing
          # gap in the stock mls policy's systemd module -- unrelated to
          # cloudform/cloud-init, and with a long history of similar
          # one-off bugs upstream (e.g. RHEL-14289, which had to add a
          # private semanage_exec_t type just so selinux-autorelabel.service
          # itself could run under mls):
          #   - systemd_selinux_autorelabel_generator_t denied `execute`
          #     on shell_exec_t (can't even exec bash)
          #   - EVERY *_generator_t domain tried so far (getty, sysv,
          #     factory-reset, bless-boot, rc-local, cryptsetup, ...)
          #     denied `write` on kmsg_device_t:s15 -- a genuine mls BLP
          #     "no write up" constraint, since these domains run at the
          #     low end (s0) of their s0-s15 range by default. This looks
          #     like a blanket mls-vs-systemd-generators design gap, not
          #     a handful of missing rules, so mark the whole family
          #     permissive dynamically instead of chasing them one by one.
          # This is a diagnostic probe, not a real fix (a real fix
          # belongs in selinux-policy's systemd module, not here).
          semanage permissive -a systemd_selinux_autorelabel_generator_t
          seinfo -t 2>/dev/null | grep -E '_generator_t$' | while read -r t; do
              semanage permissive -a "$t" 2>&1 || true
          done
          semanage permissive -l | grep generator

          # That generator probe got past the generator/kmsg denials, but
          # the *next* enforcing boot still hung: local-fs.target failed
          # (boot.mount, boot-efi.mount, home.mount, var.mount all timed
          # out waiting for their /dev/disk/by-uuid symlinks -- i.e.
          # something in the udev/blkid path isn't working under
          # enforcing), which cascades into emergency mode, which is a
          # dead end for tmt (no sshd, root locked). Console output alone
          # couldn't explain *why*: `kauditd_printk_skb: N callbacks
          # suppressed` shows the kernel was rate-limiting how many AVCs
          # it echoed to the console once auditd wasn't yet up to drain
          # them, so the actual denial(s) responsible were never visible.
          #
          # Static policy review makes the obvious suspects unlikely:
          # udev_t already carries mls_file_read_all_levels (and more),
          # and the fstools module (which udev_t domtrans's to for blkid,
          # landing in fsadm_t -- also mls_file_read_all_levels'd) is
          # enabled for mls. So this needs real evidence, not more
          # guessing from static analysis. Two changes to get it:
          #
          #  1. `nofail` + a short device-timeout on every non-swap fstab
          #     mount, so a stuck-forever by-uuid symlink can no longer
          #     block local-fs.target -> multi-user.target -> sshd. This
          #     doesn't fix anything; it just stops the symptom (hang)
          #     from hiding the cause, by letting us reach a live SSH
          #     session after the same underlying failure occurs.
          #  2. `audit_backlog_limit=8192` (default is 64) on the kernel
          #     command line, so early-boot AVCs queue up for auditd
          #     instead of being dropped once the default 64-entry
          #     backlog fills -- independent of and in addition to the
          #     console print-rate-limit above; this is what actually
          #     guarantees ausearch can find them later, regardless of
          #     what did or didn't make it to the console.
          cp /etc/fstab /etc/fstab.pre-nofail-probe

          # New (04bf1a50 follow-up): a fresh enforcing run with the
          # watchdog probe confirmed two things with sharper evidence
          # than before. First, /dev/watchdog* doesn't exist on this
          # guest at all ("No such file or directory" from both `ls`
          # and `wdctl`) -- the ~5-minute external reboot cadence seen
          # throughout this investigation is *not* a guest-visible
          # hardware/software watchdog, it's coming from outside the
          # guest OS entirely (infrastructure-level, invisible to tmt
          # and to us). Second, the by-uuid hang is still exactly what
          # blocks multi-user.target every cycle, deterministically,
          # completely independent of the separate ~2min
          # NetworkManager-wait-online slowness (which resolves on its
          # own and never blocks anything, since network-online.target
          # only Wants it, not Requires it).
          #
          # Rather than just widening the by-uuid timeout again (60s
          # already didn't help), bypass udev's by-uuid symlink
          # resolution for these mounts entirely: home/var/boot are
          # plain fstab entries on the *same* underlying block device
          # as the already-mounted root filesystem (confirmed earlier
          # -- they share one UUID, root is just several btrfs
          # subvolumes), so the raw partition device node is already
          # known to the kernel the moment it's partitioned/enumerated
          # and doesn't need udev to (re-)probe it by UUID at all. If
          # udev's per-device blkid/symlink pipeline is what's actually
          # stalling under enforcing (everything gathered so far points
          # there: the coldplug trigger itself finishes in under a
          # second every boot, but the resulting by-uuid symlink still
          # isn't there 60s later), skipping that pipeline for these
          # mounts should let them come up as fast as root itself does.
          # Resolve while still permissive, when blkid/findmnt are known
          # to work fine, so this can't itself become a chicken-and-egg
          # problem once enforcing is live.
          while read -r uuid; do
              [ -z "$uuid" ] && continue
              dev=$(blkid -U "$uuid" 2>/dev/null || true)
              if [ -n "$dev" ]; then
                  echo "resolved UUID=$uuid -> $dev, rewriting fstab"
                  sed -i "s#UUID=$uuid#$dev#g" /etc/fstab
              else
                  echo "WARNING: could not resolve UUID=$uuid via blkid, leaving as UUID= (nofail/timeout below still applies)"
              fi
          done < <(awk '!/^#/ && NF >= 4 && $3 != "swap" && $1 ~ /^UUID=/ { sub(/^UUID=/, "", $1); print $1 }' /etc/fstab.pre-nofail-probe | sort -u)

          # Keep nofail + a device-timeout as a safety net regardless
          # (covers any entry that didn't resolve above, and just
          # generally keeps a stuck mount from hard-blocking boot).
          cp /etc/fstab /etc/fstab.pre-nofail-probe2
          awk '!/^#/ && NF >= 4 && $3 != "swap" { $4 = $4",nofail,x-systemd.device-timeout=60" } { print }' \
              /etc/fstab.pre-nofail-probe2 > /etc/fstab
          echo "=== /etc/fstab after UUID->raw-device rewrite + nofail (diagnostic only) ==="
          cat /etc/fstab

          grubby --update-kernel=ALL --args="audit_backlog_limit=8192"
          echo "=== confirm audit_backlog_limit landed on the default boot entry ==="
          grubby --info=DEFAULT | grep args

          # Even with the fstab mounts made survivable above, the very next
          # boot still never got tmt's SSH connection back: local-fs.target
          # and sysinit.target *did* get reached this time (real progress --
          # confirms the nofail fix works), but basic.target/multi-user.target
          # still failed, because specific services (systemd-logind,
          # chronyd, ...) directly require var.mount/home.mount rather than
          # going through local-fs.target, and separately NetworkManager.service
          # itself hung for its own ~11-minute internal timeout. Even the
          # *root* filesystem's own by-uuid symlink -- already mounted and
          # in active use -- never got (re-)created post-switch_root, which
          # is why this looks like systemd-udevd's whole event-processing
          # path is stuck, not something storage/blkid-specific (unrelated
          # devices like /dev/ttyS0 and /dev/zram0 time out identically).
          # NetworkManager itself discovers interfaces via a udev monitor,
          # so it's plausible it's not blocked by any *specific* unmet
          # dependency at all -- it may just be waiting forever to be told
          # by udev that the NIC exists.
          #
          # Chasing each individually-blocking unit looked like a losing
          # game at the time, so this step used to also bypass udev and
          # NetworkManager for networking entirely (a raw dhclient run
          # from /sys/class/net, plus a dependency-free sshd override) to
          # reach a live shell no matter what. That escape hatch itself
          # turned out to be buggy (a bad dhclient flag, then an
          # empty-interface race) and broke two runs on its own, so it's
          # gone now -- both of those runs otherwise reached
          # multi-user.target, cloud-init.target, and a real `login:`
          # prompt with ens5 up over plain DHCP before ever touching it,
          # confirming NIC/network bring-up itself is fine. Removing it
          # was a real fix, just not *the* fix: a follow-up run without
          # the escape hatch still hung, with a much sharper signature
          # now that the escape-hatch noise is gone -- home.mount,
          # boot.mount, boot-efi.mount, and var.mount (every non-root
          # fstab entry) fail every single cycle because their
          # /dev/disk/by-uuid/<uuid>.device units time out at exactly
          # the 10s x-systemd.device-timeout set below, while the root
          # filesystem (mounted from the initrd, before fstab/udev-by-uuid
          # is even involved) is fine, and sshd.service *does* start
          # successfully around t=60s regardless -- tmt's reconnect still
          # never succeeds, though, so something about that half-booted
          # state (no /home, no /var, no /boot) still isn't enough.
          # Since the permissive probe found the same by-uuid symlink
          # (for the root partition, granted) created in ~11s with zero
          # relevant denials, the obvious next thing to try was raising
          # x-systemd.device-timeout (below) well past 10s -- but that
          # ruled the timing-cliff theory out: even at 60s, the exact
          # same single by-uuid device (41a21019..., which turns out to
          # be the *root* filesystem's own UUID -- home/boot/var are
          # plain fstab entries pointing at the same btrfs device, not
          # genuinely separate partitions) never appears, every cycle,
          # confirmed against the *complete* enforcing-boot console
          # output this time (not console-only guessing): no new AVCs
          # anywhere near it even with dontaudit still off and
          # audit_backlog_limit=8192 from the probe step, and static
          # policy review confirms udev_t already carries
          # mls_file_read_all_levels/mls_file_write_all_levels/
          # mls_process_write_down, and the base mls policy's own
          # constraint file (policy/mls) explicitly includes blk_file in
          # the same "read/getattr/execute" mlsconstrain that
          # mlsfileread exempts, and exempts ioctl/lock entirely -- so
          # this isn't a missing-rule gap in the classic sense; udev_t
          # is already about as trusted as a domain can be for exactly
          # this kind of raw-device read. That means the block (if it's
          # SELinux-related at all) is happening somewhere `ausearch`
          # can't see, or it isn't an SELinux permission problem at all
          # but a genuine udev/blkid hang specific to MLS labels.
          #
          # RESOLVED (this round): the live monitor (mls-udev-monitor.service,
          # kept in mls-cloudinit-fix/ for reference but no longer enabled
          # below -- see the masking fix a few lines down for why) never
          # actually got to show anything conclusive about a *second*
          # uevent, because the real bug was one step earlier: this same
          # boot's console showed selinux-autorelabel.service deciding yet
          # another full relabel was needed and running `fixfiles -T 0
          # restore` at ~t=6.5s, competing for I/O on the very same
          # nvme0n1p3 device the "stuck" dev-nvme0n1p3.device unit lives
          # on, for the whole ~64s window everything else was timing out
          # in. Masking that service outright (below) turned out to be
          # the actual fix: the very next run reached multi-user.target,
          # started sshd, and reached network-online.target in under two
          # minutes, with dev-nvme0n1p3.device no longer timing out at
          # all. mls-udev-diag.service/mls-udev-monitor.service did their
          # job (localizing the problem to *something* competing with
          # nvme0n1p3, which is what pointed at the relabel-restart
          # theory in the first place) and are retired now to cut console
          # noise for the next investigation below -- not deleted, in
          # case a future regression needs them again.

          sed -i 's/^SELINUX=.*/SELINUX=enforcing/' /etc/selinux/config

          # New evidence from the udevadm-diagnostic run: every enforcing
          # attempt so far -- captured this time with a complete (not
          # console-only) boot log -- shows selinux-autorelabel.service
          # deciding *another* full "SELinux mls policy relabel is
          # required" run is needed and starting `fixfiles -T 0 restore`
          # at ~t=6.5s, with no "Finished" line anywhere in the rest of
          # the captured boot (i.e. it may still be running, competing
          # for I/O on the very same nvme0n1p3 device the stuck by-uuid
          # symlink lives on, when everything else times out at t=64s).
          # /.autorelabel should have been consumed by the very first
          # relabel boot (several steps ago) and never reappear; check
          # its state now, while SSH still works, so this is visible
          # regardless of whether the upcoming enforcing boot succeeds:
          echo "=== is /.autorelabel still present before the enforcing boot? (it shouldn't be) ==="
          ls -la /.autorelabel 2>&1 || echo "/.autorelabel absent, as expected"
          echo "=== did the original relabel service actually finish successfully? ==="
          systemctl status selinux-autorelabel.service --no-pager -l 2>&1 || true
          # Confirmed (with dontaudit off this time) that clearing the
          # flag here wasn't enough: /.autorelabel is absent right here,
          # every time this step runs, and yet the *next* boot's console
          # still logs "SELinux mls policy relabel is required" and runs
          # a full `fixfiles -T 0 restore` at ~t=6.5s on every single one
          # of several power-cycled boots afterwards, each time re-mangling
          # some files' SELinux user back to unconfined_u mid-boot (the
          # same class of bug as the ld.so.cache fix above, just spreading
          # to other files -- gconv-modules.cache showed up this round)
          # and competing for I/O on the same nvme0n1p3 device that's
          # already timing out. So something other than the flag file
          # itself (most likely dracut's own early-boot SELINUXTYPE
          # mismatch check, re-run fresh from a cold kernel on every
          # boot) is what actually decides a relabel is needed -- clearing
          # /.autorelabel doesn't touch that decision at all. Stop trying
          # to out-race whatever recreates the need and just mask the
          # service outright: prepare_for_mls_force_relabel() already
          # did the equivalent full relabel once already (restorecon -RF /,
          # in the "Reboot to apply MLS and Relabel" step above), so
          # selinux-autorelabel.service running again here is pure
          # redundant I/O, not a correctness requirement.
          rm -f /.autorelabel
          systemctl mask selinux-autorelabel.service
          systemctl status selinux-autorelabel.service --no-pager -l 2>&1 || true

          # Previously (7d7eabf4) this dialed dontaudit/debug logging back
          # off here, since two earlier rounds found nothing and the extra
          # console I/O was itself a suspected contributor to instability.
          # Since then the stale-relabel bug got fixed (confirmed gone in
          # 04bf1a50's run) and NetworkManager-wait-online was cleared as a
          # red herring (it stalls but never blocks anything) -- so this is
          # a meaningfully cleaner baseline than either of those "found
          # nothing" rounds. Worth one more look with full diagnostics on,
          # specifically per the theory that this is a socket/netlink or
          # udev-worker-communication denial that a plain permissive probe
          # wouldn't reproduce (permissive doesn't actually retry/backoff
          # the way a real enforcing-mode block does): keep dontaudit off
          # and udevd debug logging live through the actual enforcing
          # attempt this time, instead of reverting to defaults beforehand.
          # Deferred with -N for the same reason as the Probe step's own
          # `semanage dontaudit -N off` above: this phase's `tmt-reboot`
          # (into enforcing, no less) is still ahead, so there's no need
          # to risk a live reload racing against it.
          semanage dontaudit -N off
          echo "=== confirm debug configs are still in place for this attempt ==="
          ls -la /etc/systemd/system/systemd-udevd.service.d/99-debug.conf \
                 /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/99-diag-console.conf 2>&1 || true
          systemctl daemon-reload

          # RESOLVED as of the masking fix above: the very next run after
          # it reached multi-user.target, started sshd.service, and reached
          # network-online.target all within ~111s (Startup finished in
          # 811ms (kernel) + 2.272s (initrd) + 1min 48.529s (userspace) --
          # console-verified, not guessed) -- dev-nvme0n1p3.device no
          # longer times out at all. That's the boot hang this whole
          # investigation was chasing, fixed for real.
          #
          # But acceptance criterion 1 ("boots reliably to a working SSH
          # session") still isn't met: tmt's own reconnect never once
          # succeeded across four ~5-minute power-cycles of the guest
          # (still the same pre-existing, external, tmt/Artemis-independent
          # cadence noted above -- unrelated to this fix), and tmt-run.log
          # shows exactly why -- not an SSH handshake/auth failure, but a
          # bare TCP-level failure on *every* attempt:
          #   ssh: connect to host <ip> port 22: Connection timed out
          # That message comes from the client's own connect() call, before
          # any SSH protocol exchange even starts, and it's the same result
          # whether the attempt happens to fall inside the guest's
          # confirmed-live window or not. Since the same fixed IP accepted
          # SSH connections without issue for every earlier *permissive*
          # reboot in this exact run (see the rsync/tmt-topology-copy
          # activity right before this step), something specific to this
          # enforcing boot -- not a general Testing Farm/network problem --
          # is making the guest's SSH port externally unreachable even
          # though sshd starts successfully *inside* it. ausearch/dontaudit
          # off shows nothing network-related, so this doesn't look like an
          # SELinux denial either. Leading theories, in rough order of
          # suspicion: (a) a missing/late default route -- inbound SYNs
          # could still arrive and get a SYN-ACK queued, but with no route
          # back out that SYN-ACK never reaches the client, which looks
          # exactly like a client-side connect timeout; (b) a firewall
          # (nftables/firewalld) ruleset that ends up default-deny for a
          # window, or permanently, on this boot specifically; (c) sshd
          # somehow not actually bound where expected despite logging
          # "Started". A loopback self-connect from *inside* the guest
          # would distinguish (c) from (a)/(b) instantly, and doesn't need
          # external reachability at all. Added mls-net-diag.service to
          # test all three at once, forced to the console every 15s for
          # 5 minutes (spans a full power-cycle) since SSH is exactly
          # what's unavailable to check this any other way.
          cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/mls-net-diag.service \
              /etc/systemd/system/mls-net-diag.service
          systemctl daemon-reload
          systemctl enable mls-net-diag.service

          # Ran it (Testing Farm eb2f649e): the loopback self-connect
          # answered (c) immediately -- sshd accepts on 127.0.0.1:22
          # every single one of 20+ iterations across the full 5-minute
          # window, so sshd itself is fine. But `ip route show` came back
          # completely empty and `ip -4 addr show` showed only lo, which
          # would seem to confirm theory (a) (no route/address at all) --
          # except the *same* boot's console also shows the diagnostic's
          # own `ss`/`systemctl` calls failing outright ("Cannot open
          # netlink socket: Permission denied", "Failed to retrieve unit
          # state: Access denied", the latter with an explicit
          # `scon=initrc_t tcon=init_t ... perm=status ... Permission
          # denied`). A plain ad hoc systemd service with no configured
          # label lands in initrc_t under mls, which the stock policy
          # doesn't grant NETLINK_ROUTE socket or systemd-status query
          # access to -- so the empty `ip route`/`ip addr` output is (most
          # likely) this diagnostic being blind, not evidence the route
          # table is actually empty. Mark initrc_t permissive for this
          # probe only (same pattern already used for the generator-domain
          # probe above) so the *next* run's mls-net-diag.service output
          # can actually be trusted either way.
          semanage permissive -a initrc_t

          # Ran it again (Testing Farm b5719bd8) with initrc_t now
          # permissive, so this round's mls-net-diag output can finally be
          # trusted: `ip -4 -o addr show`/`ip route show` came back
          # completely empty (lo only) across all 20 iterations spanning
          # the whole 5-minute window -- not blind this time (no new
          # netlink-related AVCs), so the guest genuinely has no non-loopback
          # address or route at all, confirming theory (a) for real.
          #
          # Cross-referencing the full console log (not just mls-net-diag's
          # own output) found the actual mechanism, and it's bigger than a
          # missing route: dev-nvme0n1p2.device AND dev-nvme0n1p3.device
          # *both* still time out every boot (contradicting the "fixed for
          # real" note above -- that was one lucky run, not a real fix), but
          # boot now survives it because nothing hard-requires them. More
          # importantly, dev-ttyS0.device and dev-zram0.device -- a serial
          # console and an in-memory swap device, nothing to do with disks,
          # blkid, or nvme at all -- *also* time out in the exact same
          # ~45-second window right after systemd-udevd starts, during which
          # udevd's own debug log (systemd-udevd.service.d/99-debug.conf)
          # shows zero device/worker activity whatsoever. That rules out an
          # nvme- or blkid-specific bug: this is udev's coldplug event
          # processing stalling *system-wide*, for every device type, for
          # ~45-65s immediately after udevd starts, on every single
          # enforcing boot. Most device units happen to survive it (their
          # dependents tolerate the delay or the timeout isn't fatal to
          # boot), but the network interface does not: the kernel brings up
          # `ena 0000:00:05.0` and renames it to ens5 exactly once, during
          # the initrd stage (~t=5.9s, before switch-root, which is also
          # when cloud-init's initrd-stage network bring-up briefly gives it
          # 172.31.19.49 to fetch instance metadata) -- but NetworkManager
          # in the *main* OS, for the entire boot, only ever logs "(lo): new
          # Loopback device" and never once logs discovering ens5 at all.
          # No AVC (permissive or enforcing, dontaudit already off) implicates
          # udev_t or NetworkManager_t specifically, so this doesn't look like
          # a classic missing-TE-rule gap either -- but that doesn't rule out
          # a dontaudit-independent enforcement path (e.g. a slow/blocked
          # avc_has_perm() computation under MLS's s0-s15:c0.c1023 range that
          # never generates a denial because the operation isn't actually
          # denied, just pathologically delayed). Mark both permissive, same
          # pattern as initrc_t above, to test that theory directly: if the
          # coldplug stall and/or ens5 discovery clear up with these
          # permissive, that's strong evidence MLS-specific SID/context
          # computation overhead for these two heavily-invoked domains is the
          # real mechanism, not a discrete missing rule.
          #
          # First attempt (b98aa32f) used a live `semanage permissive -a`
          # for both, same as the initrc_t precedent above. That precedent
          # is safe for initrc_t (an ad hoc oneshot diagnostic domain with
          # no long-running daemon), but NetworkManager_t is the domain of
          # the *actively running* NetworkManager process that owns the
          # network connection this very SSH session (and the upcoming
          # tmt-reboot handshake) depends on. `semanage permissive -a`
          # without `-n` does an immediate rebuild-and-reload of the whole
          # mls store into the live kernel, and Testing Farm runs
          # 2fc81438/548f9fc8/0a61c365 all confirm the fallout: every one
          # died with "Failed to push workdir to the guest" / "Plugin
          # raised an unhandled exception" *immediately* after this step's
          # tmt-reboot call -- too fast to be a real boot timeout, and the
          # same failure the semodule-install comment below already warned
          # about for exactly this reason ("the guest broke immediately
          # ... because the live kernel was suddenly enforcing MLS
          # semantics no running context actually satisfied"). Marking
          # NetworkManager_t's own domain permissive live, on a session
          # NetworkManager itself is carrying, is the same class of
          # self-inflicted disruption. `-n` defers the store update to disk
          # without an immediate reload -- harmless here since a
          # `tmt-reboot` is the very next line anyway, so the kernel picks
          # up the change fresh on that reboot instead of hot-reloading
          # into the live, still-in-use session.
          semanage permissive -n -a udev_t
          semanage permissive -n -a NetworkManager_t

          # Force a full, fresh local -> init -> config -> final cloud-init
          # run under enforcing on the next boot (see the "Reset cloud-init's
          # per-instance state" comment in the first prepare step -- without
          # this, cloud-init would just see the instance as already
          # provisioned and skip everything).
          cloud-init clean --logs

          tmt-reboot -t 1200
      fi

  - name: Investigate the enforcing-boot disk-by-uuid timeout (udev/blkid MLS theory)
    how: shell
    script: |
      if [ "${TEST_ENFORCING:-1}" = "1" ]; then
          echo "=== did sshd/networking come up normally this boot? ==="
          systemctl status sshd.service --no-pager 2>&1 || true
          ip addr show 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== did local-fs.target / basic.target / multi-user.target fail again this boot? ==="
          systemctl status local-fs.target basic.target multi-user.target --no-pager 2>&1 || true
          systemctl list-jobs 2>&1 || true
          systemctl --failed --no-pager 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== restore fstab (nofail was diagnostic-only) ==="
          if [ -f /etc/fstab.pre-nofail-probe ]; then
              mv /etc/fstab.pre-nofail-probe /etc/fstab
          fi

          echo "=== full AVC list, this boot (should now be complete: audit_backlog_limit=8192) ==="
          ausearch -m AVC,USER_AVC -ts boot 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== specifically: anything denied on a fixed_disk_device_t / blk_file, and who ==="
          ausearch -m AVC -ts boot 2>&1 | grep -E 'blk_file|fixed_disk_device_t' || echo "none found"

          echo "=== is udev_t (or whatever ran blkid) actually mlsfileread-trusted in the *loaded* policy? ==="
          seinfo -a mlsfileread -x 2>&1 | grep -E 'udev_t|fsadm_t|lvm_t' || echo "none of udev_t/fsadm_t/lvm_t carry mlsfileread in the loaded policy"

          echo "=== what actually ran systemd-udevd / blkid, and in which domain ==="
          ps -eZ 2>&1 | grep -E 'udev|blkid' || true
          journalctl -b -u systemd-udevd --no-pager 2>&1 || true

          echo "=== do the by-uuid symlinks exist now (after nofail let boot continue)? ==="
          ls -la /dev/disk/by-uuid/ 2>&1 || true
          for u in $(ls /dev/disk/by-uuid/ 2>/dev/null); do
              blkid /dev/disk/by-uuid/"$u" 2>&1 || true
          done

          echo "=== try running blkid directly against the raw disk, see what it hits live ==="
          lsblk -no NAME,UUID 2>&1 || true
      fi

execute:
  how: tmt
  script: |
    echo "=== SELinux status ==="
    getenforce
    sestatus

    echo "=== cloud-init result from this boot ==="
    cloud-init status --long || true

    echo "=== cloud-init unit activity from this boot ==="
    journalctl -b -u 'cloud-init*' --no-pager || true

    # Earlier runs showed the main /usr/bin/cloud-init binary correctly
    # transitioning into cloud_init_t. But the cloud-init-generator systemd
    # generator (/usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/cloud-init-generator --
    # NOTE: earlier versions of this diagnostic had the wrong filename,
    # "cloud-init.local-generator", which never existed, so this check was
    # silently a no-op) is observed running as scontext=initrc_t instead of
    # cloud_init_t, even though cloudform.fc already matches it via the
    # same `cloud-init.*` pattern as the main binary. That process is also
    # seen hitting the already-known /etc/ld.so.cache unlabeled_t denial
    # in the very same boot -- suggesting this generator is just another
    # casualty of the same relabel-race bug (some files not getting their
    # real label applied before early-boot processes need them), rather
    # than a distinct selinux-policy .te gap. These checks confirm that:
    #   1) whether the generator's on-disk label actually matches what
    #      cloudform.fc expects (cloud_init_exec_t) or not, and
    #   2) whether a transition rule exists from *either* init_t or
    #      initrc_t (systemd may invoke generators from either, depending
    #      on version/config) to cloud_init_t, in case the label is fine
    #      but the transition is scoped to the wrong source domain.
    echo "=== cloud-init entrypoint labels: on-disk vs policy-expected ==="
    ls -Z /usr/bin/cloud-init /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/cloud-init-generator 2>&1 || true
    matchpathcon -V /usr/bin/cloud-init /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/cloud-init-generator /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/99-preserve-ssh-host-keys.cfg /etc/ld.so.cache 2>&1 || true
    restorecon -nv /usr/bin/cloud-init /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/cloud-init-generator /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/99-preserve-ssh-host-keys.cfg /etc/ld.so.cache 2>&1 || true

    echo "=== does the loaded MLS policy define an init_t or initrc_t -> cloud_init_t transition? ==="
    sesearch -A -s init_t -t cloud_init_exec_t -c file -p execute 2>&1 || true
    sesearch -T -s init_t -t cloud_init_exec_t -c process 2>&1 || true
    sesearch -A -s initrc_t -t cloud_init_exec_t -c file -p execute 2>&1 || true
    sesearch -T -s initrc_t -t cloud_init_exec_t -c process 2>&1 || true

    echo "=== actual domain cloud-init is running in (should be cloud_init_t) ==="
    ps -eZ | grep -i cloud || true

    echo "=== CAPTURED AVC DENIALS (this boot) ==="
    ausearch -m AVC,USER_AVC -ts boot || true

    echo "=== Suggested policy for observed denials ==="
    ausearch -m AVC -ts boot | audit2allow || true

    # Relabel-race investigation: does /.autorelabel even get consumed on
    # this image? systemd/dracut's autorelabel handling deletes the flag
    # file itself once the relabel completes -- if it's still present
    # after boot, the relabel service never ran at all, which would
    # explain the unlabeled_t denials far better than a race between
    # relabel and early boot units.
    echo "=== is /.autorelabel still present after boot? (it should have been consumed) ==="
    ls -la /.autorelabel 2>&1 || echo "/.autorelabel absent (consumed, as expected)"

    echo "=== does a selinux-autorelabel(-mark).service unit exist / did it run? ==="
    systemctl list-unit-files 'selinux-autorelabel*' 2>&1 || true
    systemctl status selinux-autorelabel.service 2>&1 || true
    journalctl -b -u 'selinux-autorelabel*' --no-pager -o short-monotonic 2>&1 || true

    echo "=== full early-boot unit timeline, for correlating relabel vs cloud-init start ==="
    journalctl -b --no-pager -o short-monotonic | head -200 || true

    echo "=== exact epoch timestamps: first unlabeled_t AVC vs cloud-init-local.service start ==="
    ausearch -m AVC -ts boot 2>/dev/null | grep -m1 unlabeled_t || true
    journalctl -b -u cloud-init-local.service --no-pager -o short-precise 2>&1 | head -5 || true
